I could be helpful ah screw it the max  this series was one of the things that  completely blew open my mind in terms of  what can be done with animation I saw it  around the same time that I saw Ralph  Bochy's Wizards and the resulting  one-two punch really cemented that my  love of animation was not going to fade  as I crossed the threshold of  adolescence and entered into adulthood  so how does one describe the max I'm not  asking that rhetorically I genuinely  have no idea I suppose bizarre would be  the word of the day everything from the  ourselves the plot it's all very  experimental it's borderline stream of  consciousness but it does sort of follow  this internal logic so it's weird as  heck but it's not weird for just the  sake of being weird which keeps it from  being as pretentious as it really could  have ended up being superficially it's a  superhero story or at least it starts  out as one there's a costumed hero  there's a damsel there's a villa in  reality it's about family ties spirit  animals astral projections dreams versus  reality and emotional suppression just  to mention a few of the major themes so  given how difficult the stories to  describe I suppose we'll talk about the  animation style and there really is  nothing else like this a lot of the  artwork was quite literally scanned from  Sam Keith's original comic book pages  but it doesn't mean that it's like those  motion comics that's kind of get put out  every now and then there's some real  dynamic animation going on now whenever  you do something in animation the more  details there are and something the  harder it is to make it move and make it  look good when it does so the way the  show maintains the integrity of some  incredibly highly detailed images is by  only animating a few select parts of the  image now that keeps it from becoming  overly static the most common example  would be a character who's speaking and  overall they're not moving but their  eyes are squinting and adjusting in  their mouth is moving but the rest of  the highly detailed images left pretty  much there another fairly prominent  example is the female character may be  standing pose but her hair is waving  behind her in the wind and sort of  keeping that steady motion doesn't ever  make it feel like you're just looking at  a static page or an image that isn't  moving it's sort of the way that  balances  maintain is because so few elements are  actually in motion it means they were  able to put a lot of work and effort  into making sure the ones that were  moving look amazing and look as good as  the stuff that's saying still if they  are trying to animate every single  aspect of the character the way the way  they animate these select elements they  never would have had the time or the  money to do it another way they come  back the static frame issues just by  really clever use of the camera mainly  in the form of pans you'll have the  camera slowly move across an image and  just revealing more as it goes as a  character is talking and it keeps it  from feeling locked off but then at the  same time doesn't require the extra time  effort and money required to actually  animate the characters moving all that  much because the camera gives that  feeling of movement it's a technique you  extra see all the time and animate but  off the top my head I can't think of a  Western animated film that has borrowed  that technique but has zero anime  influence in the art style now I'm a fan  of the comic book as well although I saw  the animated show first but I actually  think that the n main show works better  in some respects than the comic book  does particularly when it comes to  flashing back and forth between sort of  the real world and the dream world of  the outback there's one shot which burns  so strongly into my memory when I first  saw it it's still amazing it's this  incredibly long slow pan across the city  and they'll just be flashes of it  becoming the Outback so like for  instance you'll pan across and you'll  see this is father heavy guy in a chair  with his kids crawling all over and  suddenly it'll flash to a carcass with  scavengers gnawing on authentic flashes  back to reality again as the camera  continues to pan it's an amazing shot  and it just integrates these two worlds  and draws the parallels better than the  comic book page did then there are the  characters who are as unique as the  artwork max himself he is a really hero  he's more just kind of a schlub and he  knows he has to protect Julie winters  but he doesn't really understand how and  he hasn't even bothered to question why  Julie herself is gorgeously flawed  ultimately this really is much more her  story than it is Max's and she's a very  well-rounded and very complicated  character it is all  quite refreshing that she isn't drawn as  the physical ideal there's a tension  brought a number of times to a slight  gut that she has that she probably wish  wasn't there but the way the images are  drawn there's still a love of her form  of her more realistically proportioned  body and that's just really nice to see  and then there's mr. gone who is the de  facto villain of the piece and beyond  just an amazing design which is across  the board on this thing but the voice  work done by the late Barry Stigler is  beyond compare this is a character who  likes to talk and he does it a lot and  he could have very easily been boring or  even grading in the hands of the wrong  voice actor but I never get tired of  hearing him talk which is a good thing  because he serves as narrator at various  points across the show gone is also an  interesting villain because he really  blurs lions in terms of how much of a  villain he really is I mean he's a bad  person there's really no question about  that but at the same time he is offering  more guidance and more insight to Julie  and what she's going through than anyone  else in the story and it's a very  complicated very fascinating dynamic  this is the sort of show that I wish I  could recommend to everybody but I'm  good conscious I can't because it's just  too weird and some people they're just  gonna be turned off by how strange this  thing is however I'll say this if you  have a love of animation eyes on art  form and you haven't seen this before  watch the first 30 minutes which is  roughly the first two episodes if you  feel it's too weird for you at that  point you can walk away and still feel  confident that you've experienced the  visual uniqueness that is the max but  who knows maybe those 30 minutes will  hook you the way that they hooked me and  you'll dig this thing from start to  finish so were you around for the days  when MTV got into experimental animation  the era that gave us the max because it  also gave us things like aeon flux the  head even the first incarnation of  beavis and butt-head if you were around  and saw any of these what was your  favorite of that kind of error and realm  of television whatever it is drop  something down in the comments and let's  talk about it as for me I'll be back as  soon as I can get my next fix
 

